libertarians regardless of what they say  when they're sort of spelling out the  abstract of ideas of their philosophy  when they try to convince people of  libertarianism they do it by talking  about the way in which free markets make  life better for the poor my name is Matt  Zielinski I'm an associate professor of  philosophy here at the University of San  Diego and co-director of the Institute  for law and philosophy here I'm also the  founder of the bleeding heart  libertarians blog a bleeding heart  libertarian is somebody who like all  libertarians believes in free markets  believes in private property individual  liberty strictly limited government one  of the things that's distinctive about  bleeding-heart libertarianism is that it  takes a commitment to social justice to  be a core element in the justification  of libertarianism that's why we believe  in libertarianism and free markets in  private property so if you believe in  social justice what you believe then is  that if political and economic  institutions do not allow the poor and  vulnerable members of society to live  decent lives then that counts against  counts strongly against the Justice of  those institutions but simply being  committed to social justice does not  mean that you're committed to the view  that government must directly try to  promote the well-being of the poor and  vulnerable members of society one of the  great insights of libertarians and this  is something that we bleeding-heart  libertarians don't disagree with it all  is that the welfare of the poor and  vulnerable is often better promoted by  lays a fair if it turned out that we  were absolutely wrong about all of this  we'd give up libertarianism we don't  think that's very likely to happen what  might be likely to happen though is that  you could demonstrate that under some  circumstances completely free markets  that is 0 government intervention in the  economy 0 government assistance to poor  or working people it might be possible  to demonstrate that that is worse from  the perspective of social justice than  something  lightly more expanded right so a state  with a social safety net that provided  some relief for poor who couldn't  support themselves on the market or find  support from voluntary charity so I  think there's an openness among  bleeding-heart libertarians to a social  safety net and again here we're online  with I think the mainstream of the  libertarian intellectual tradition if  you look at people like Adam Smith or  John Locke Friedrich Hayek Milton  Friedman all of them supported some kind  of social safety net some minimal role  of government beyond merely protecting  the rights the negative libertarian  rights of its citizens there's two kinds  of things we're trying to do with this  blog in terms of outreach to the left  one of them is a kind of strategic goal  right just winning political allies for  liberty where we can find them but most  of us are academic philosophers or  political theorists and political  strategy is not really are a strong suit  so a bigger part of what we're trying to  do on this blog is really just an  intellectual exercise we're trying to  develop the ideas and follow the ideas  where we think they lead us and I think  if you look at the kinds of core ideas  of libertarianism what it is that drives  people to libertarianism right what you  find is that a lot of those ideas are  shared by the left libertarians  regardless of what they say when they're  sort of spelling out the abstract of  ideas of their philosophy when they try  to convince people of libertarianism  they do it by talking about the way in  which free markets make life better for  the poor the way in which days expand  opportunities for all people not just  the privileged few and so that idea is  doing some real work in the  philosophical justification of  libertarianism even in people who don't  acknowledge it even in people I think  like an R and or Robert Nozick and we're  trying to latch onto those ideas show  that they're an important building block  in the justification of libertarianism  and then follow to them to their  conclusion where do those ideas really  lead what direction philosophically do  they push libertarianism in if we  recognize them and take them to their  logical conclusion  so it's been a surprisingly positive set  of reactions on to the blog as a whole  libertarians have mostly reacted fondly  there are some libertarians who are  strongly opposed to the idea of social  justice and think that any attempt to  sort of reconcile with that particular  concept which is of course traditionally  associated with the political left is  misguided conceptually incoherent  strategically bankrupt or what have you  but I think that's a minority reaction  most people have thought it's a good  thing too it's a nice philosophical  development and and a good outreach to  people who are potential allies to  libertarians there's a kind of moderate  left which has been very receptive to  the ideas they've I don't know that  they've changed their mind on the  minimum wage laws I don't know if  they've changed their nine down  sweatshop labor it's not obvious that  people's minds can change on those  issues simply by being exposed to a few  philosophical arguments on a blog but  they're receptive to the idea that  libertarians aren't as they imagine them  to be these cold-hearted beasts who will  sort of cling to their wallets in the  face of a starving child who needs a few  pennies to get by for the next day I  think they've they've recognized that  libertarians are different than that  that libertarians really do take issues  of social justice seriously we just have  very different thoughts about what it  would take in terms of public policy to  achieve the same results that a lot of  people on the Left say they want  I think there is a subset of  libertarianism that is really opposed to  this idea of bleeding heart  libertarianism it does have more in  common with I think the popular  perception of libertarians as  indifferent to issues of social justice  I think you find elements of that in  Iran I think you find perhaps more of  that in someone like Murray Rothbard  historically maybe you'd pick out  Herbert Spencer but I think that's an  aberration that's not the mainstream of  the libertarian intellectual tradition  so if you look back at the people that  libertarians have picked out as  intellectual icons people like John  Locke people like Adam Smith people like  Friedrich Hayek or Milton Friedman none  of these people reject social justice  all of them talk about the way in which  free markets serve the interests of the  poor and working classes and all that I  think when you look closely at their  texts make that an important element  again in the justification of  libertarianism of why it is that  libertarianism is a good thing  people on the political left have often  thought that capitalism is unjust that  it's exploitative that it's unfair that  it's degrading to workers and  libertarians disagree with those claims  but we don't disagree with the  underlying moral plans we don't disagree  with a claim that it's bad for somebody  to be exploitative right or that it's  bad for somebody to be degraded what we  disagree with is a more empirical claim  it's due markets in fact do that and we  think they don't and we think we have  good empirical evidence that supports  our side of that story so I think  there's room to convince people on the  Left that they're mistaken not in their  fundamental moral commitments  necessarily although libertarians will  have some issues with those as well but  in their empirical belief in their  beliefs about what happens when free  markets operate or whether we have free  markets right now there's this  widespread belief that America is a free  market country and so people on the Left  will sort of point to the United States  and they'll say well look that's what  happens when you have a free market and  this is just false right nobody on the  libertarian movement or the libertarian  intellectual tradition points to the  United States as an icon of what it is  we're trying to achieve there's simply  no good reason to believe that the  axiomatic principles of self-ownership  are as self-evident as rothbard thought  or that we can simply derive out  property rights from those axiomatic  principles as neatly and cleanly as he  thought morality is messy we think there  are a lot of things that we care about  morally speaking we care about justice  we care about autonomy we care about  giving people what they deserve but we  also care about meeting people's needs  there's good reason to want government  to be bound by rules that are in a sense  not reflective of the entire complexity  and nuance of the moral universe you  want government to be governed by clear  and simple and foreseeable rules  people know in advance what they can get  away with without getting into the  clutches of the law  you
 

